## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical, beginner-friendly observability implementation: instrumenting a new service end-to-end with minimal changes and validating traces in a production UI.

## Key Concepts
- OpenTelemetry auto-instrumentation
- Trace-context propagation and log correlation
- Lightweight instrumentation for HTTP and database spans
- OTLP exporter configuration and backend dashboard validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal Node example: enable auto-instrumentation
// Install: @opentelemetry/api @opentelemetry/sdk-node @opentelemetry/instrumentation-http
import { NodeSDK } from '@opentelemetry/sdk-node';
import { getNodeAutoInstrumentations } from '@opentelemetry/auto-instrumentations-node';

const sdk = new NodeSDK({
  instrumentations: [getNodeAutoInstrumentations()],
  serviceName: 'accounts-service',
  traceExporter: new OTLPTraceExporter()
});
sdk.start();

// Trace ID in logs
import { trace } from '@opentelemetry/api';
app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const traceId = trace.getActiveSpan()?.spanContext().traceId;
  console.log(`Request ${req.method} ${req.path} [trace=${traceId}]`);
  next();
});
```

## Validation Plan
- Configure OTLP exporter to your backend (Jaeger/Tempo/Grafana)
- Verify HTTP request spans appear in dashboard
- Confirm database query spans are captured
- Check trace IDs appear in structured logs
- Test end-to-end with synthetic request and validate trace flow

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design isolation, backpressure controls, and controlled degradation under burst traffic, with clear rollback criteria and observability.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant isolation via edge throttling and per-tenant queues
- Backpressure mechanisms and circuit breakers
- Safe degradation strategies and cache fallbacks
- Telemetry: p95/p99 latency, error rate, queue depth, saturation
- Clear rollback criteria and automation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple token bucket limiter
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, capacity) {
    this.rate = rate;
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  allow() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= 1) {
      this.tokens -= 1;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate plan with load tests and synthetic surges?
- What metrics and thresholds would trigger a rollback, and how would you automate it?

## Why This Is Asked
Probes real-world SRE orchestration for multi-tenant systems under burst traffic, requiring dynamic budgets, fairness, and controlled degradation.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant SLOs and budgets
- Fusion of signals: latency, error rate, queue depth
- Admission control and load shedding
- Safe rollback plan with tenant-level validation
- Observability and audit logging

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code for tenant budget enforcement
function allocate(tenantId, req) {
  const budget = getBudget(tenantId);
  if (budget.remaining <= 0) return reject(req, "budget exhausted");
  // consume
  budget.consume(1);
  return forward(req);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate budget fairness under mixed workloads?
- How would you test the load-shedding policy without impacting real tenants?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes a beginner-friendly angle on data integrity under deploy pressure, focusing on per-tenant observability, controlled backpressure, and data replay safety.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant observability: lag, offsets, retry counts
- Ingress throttling and traffic-splitting
- Idempotent processing and dedup keys
- Rollback and data validation windows

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple deduplicated idempotent processor
const seen = new Set();
function handle(event){
  const id = event.tenant + ":" + event.id;
  if (seen.has(id)) return;
  seen.add(id);
  // process event
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test the rollback plan in a staging env?
- What telemetry would you add to verify data integrity after rollback?
